-- title:
--   Two minimal primes of (p) on the finite chart, oriented
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a nonzero $M$ with $p \mid M$ but $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit that reduces to $1$ under the map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$; assume the Laurent series `jqModC ℚ` (that is, $q^{-1}$ times the integral $j$-numerator series) lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`. Let $\mathfrak{X}$ be an instance of the model structure `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` over the base `R p`, and let $W_0$ be a valuation subring of $F =$ `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ (ΓM M H)` pinned by the condition that $f_0 \in W_0$ exactly when $f_0 a' = a$ as Laurent series for some power series $a, a'$ over [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) (the rationals with denominator coprime to $p$) with $a'$ not reducing to $0$ modulo $p$; this is the Gauss valuation ring. Then there are ideals $P_0, P_1$ of the finite-chart algebra `chartAlgFin p (ΓM M H) hj` such that both are minimal primes of the ideal generated by $p$, they are distinct, they are all the minimal primes of that ideal, $P_0$ is the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W_0$ (an element $b$ lies in $P_0$ iff its image in $F$ lies in `W₀.nonunits`), and: for every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit in $A$ and with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, every ring map $\rho :$ `R p` $\to A$ compatible with the structure map to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, every $i \in \{0,1\}$, every point $y$ of the fibre of the model over the residue map of $\rho$, and every prime $\mathfrak{q}$ of the finite-chart algebra whose associated point of the model equals the image of $y$ under the first projection, one has $y$ in the range of the base map of the $i$-th component morphism `𝔛.comp A hA ρ hρ i` if and only if $P_i \subseteq \mathfrak{q}$.
--
--   This describes the special fibre at $p$ of the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_H(M)$ for $p$ exactly dividing $M$ on the chart where $j$ is regular: the ideal $(p)$ has exactly two minimal primes, the one cutting out the component through the cusp $\infty$ being the centre of the Gauss ($q$-expansion) valuation, and membership of a geometric point in a component is equivalent to containment of the corresponding prime. It is used in the further analysis of points lying on one component and not the other.
